\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Reasoning language models have turned inference-time computation into a
decision variable. For the same query, a model may be allowed to reason for a
few hundred tokens or for several thousand before committing to an answer.
Increasing this budget provides additional opportunities to reconsider an
intermediate conclusion, recover from an error, or solve a difficult problem.
It can also consume computation without changing the answer, or even replace a
previously correct answer with an incorrect one. The central deployment
question is therefore not simply whether more reasoning improves average
performance, but \emph{when an additional block of reasoning is worth its cost
for the current query}.

The simplest solution is to choose one global reasoning budget and apply it to
every query. Such a policy is efficient only when queries benefit from
additional reasoning in approximately the same way. If some problems are
resolved reliably with short trajectories while others benefit from
substantially more computation, a fixed budget must either waste compute on
the former or under-allocate it to the latter. The potential value of adaptive
test-time computation therefore rests on a basic empirical premise:
\emph{the value of additional reasoning must vary meaningfully across queries
and across stages of a trajectory}. Whether this opportunity exists should be
measured rather than assumed.

A natural alternative is to inspect the current trajectory and stop when the
model appears sufficiently certain. Existing approaches use quantities such as
predictive confidence, entropy, self-consistency, answer convergence, or the
stability of intermediate predictions. These quantities can provide useful
information about the trajectory, but they do not directly answer the compute
allocation question. A high-confidence answer can still be wrong and may be
corrected by further reasoning. Conversely, even a currently correct answer
can be altered by continuation. The decision to continue therefore depends not
only on the state of the current prediction, but on the \emph{counterfactual
value of additional computation}.

This distinction is particularly important when a labeled calibration set is
not available at deployment time. Let $\Hist_{\budget}$ denote everything
observable from a reasoning trajectory up to budget $\budget$, including the
query, generated reasoning, logits, current answer, and predictive
distribution. Without an additional assumption linking these observables to
the unknown ground-truth label, correctness is not identified by
$\Hist_{\budget}$. In Section~\ref{sec:impossibility}, we formalize this point
with a two-world construction: the same distribution of observable histories
can coexist with opposite correctness assignments. Consequently, no function
of an unlabeled single trajectory can generally provide non-trivial
distribution-free control of current correctness. Confidence, stability, and
self-consistency may characterize properties of the model trajectory, but they
cannot by themselves certify that the trajectory is correct.

Rather than constructing a more elaborate proxy for current correctness, we
change the target of the decision. Consider a globally fixed grid of reasoning
budgets
\begin{equation}
\budget_1 < \budget_2 < \cdots < \budget_K .
\end{equation}
For item $i$, let $\Brier_{i,j}$ denote a proper scoring loss at checkpoint
$\budget_j$, and let $\cost_{i,j}$ denote its realised compute. We define the
marginal value of continuing from $\budget_j$ to $\budget_{j+1}$ as
\begin{equation}
\margval_j(\lambda)
=
\Exp\!\left[\Brier_{i,j}-\Brier_{i,j+1}\right]
-
\lambda\,
\Exp\!\left[\cost_{i,j+1}-\cost_{i,j}\right],
\label{eq:intro-marginal-value}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda \geq 0$ represents a user-specified exchange rate between
predictive quality and compute. The resulting decision is not
\emph{``Is the current answer correct?''}, but
\emph{``Is another block of reasoning worth its cost?''}

This reformulation is useful because continuation value can be learned from
outcomes that arrive after the original deployment decision has already been
made. Many reasoning workloads naturally provide such delayed but verifiable
feedback. Mathematical answers can eventually be checked, programs can be
executed against tests, formal proofs can be verified, and multiple-choice or
other adjudicated decisions can later receive labels. These settings make it
possible to update a reasoning-budget policy from the deployment stream itself,
without reserving a separate labeled subset purely for calibration. We refer to
this setting as \emph{held-out-free prequential compute selection}.

The remaining difficulty is counterfactual. When a policy stops an item at
budget $\budget_j$, the prediction that would have been obtained at
$\budget_{j+1}$ is normally unobserved. We address this problem using
randomized trajectory audits. A controlled subset of arriving queries is
continued to a longer or maximum budget, revealing the sequence of checkpoint
predictions that candidate stopping policies would have encountered. Once the
delayed label arrives, these audited trajectories can be used to evaluate the
predictive loss and compute consumption of multiple policies on the same item.
Because both the audit process and the selected policy may evolve over time,
the corresponding statistical procedure must remain valid under continuous
monitoring and adaptive decisions.

We introduce \textbf{MVT-CS}---\emph{Marginal Value of Thinking with Confidence
Sequences}---for this purpose. MVT-CS combines randomized continuation,
importance-weighted evaluation, and simultaneous anytime-valid confidence
sequences to compare a pre-specified family of reasoning-budget policies using
deployment feedback. The procedure does not reserve a held-out calibration
split and does not update the parameters of the underlying language model or
train an auxiliary confidence predictor. Instead, it updates estimates of
policy risk and continuation value as labeled outcomes accumulate. Importantly,
the cost of randomized exploration is included in the total compute accounting;
a stopping procedure that saves compute only by excluding the cost of learning
its policy would provide a misleading deployment comparison.

The proposed framework is useful only if adaptive compute allocation has
something to exploit. We therefore place the empirical audit before the final
evaluation of MVT-CS. First, we measure how reasoning budget changes accuracy,
Brier score, negative log-likelihood, predictive confidence, and answer
transitions such as correct-to-wrong and wrong-to-correct flips. Second, we
compare the best global fixed budget with an item-adaptive oracle to quantify
the maximum compute-selection opportunity available in each model--dataset
setting. Third, we test whether information observable at an intermediate
checkpoint---including confidence, entropy, margin, answer stability, and
confidence dynamics---is predictive of the gain from an additional reasoning
block. Only when these conditions hold does a sophisticated adaptive stopping
policy have a meaningful advantage over a fixed budget.

This ordering also makes the study falsifiable. If nearly all queries prefer
the same budget, then a well-chosen fixed policy is sufficient and
instance-adaptive stopping is unnecessary. If continuation value varies but
cannot be predicted from the current trajectory, then signal-based adaptive
stopping has limited scope even though global online budget adaptation may
remain useful. Conversely, if both substantial item-level heterogeneity and
predictive prefix information exist, reasoning-budget selection becomes a
genuine sequential decision problem. Our experimental design is intended to
distinguish these regimes rather than presuppose the most favorable one.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
Our contributions are fourfold.

\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Non-identifiability of label-free correctness.}
We formalize why current correctness cannot, without additional structural
assumptions, be identified from an unlabeled single reasoning trajectory. This
result motivates shifting the target of stopping from current correctness to
the value of continuation.

\item \textbf{Marginal-value formulation of test-time reasoning.}
We formulate each additional reasoning block as a discrete continuation
decision whose value combines improvement in a proper prediction loss with its
realised compute cost. For the Brier score, this formulation further admits a
reliability--resolution decomposition that separates changes in probabilistic
reliability from changes in discrimination.

\item \textbf{Held-out-free prequential budget selection.}
We introduce MVT-CS, which combines randomized trajectory audits, delayed
deployment feedback, importance-weighted policy evaluation, and anytime-valid
confidence sequences to compare and update a finite family of reasoning-budget
policies without reserving a separate calibration split.

\item \textbf{Empirical audit of reasoning-budget opportunity.}
We evaluate how reasoning budget affects predictive quality and answer dynamics,
quantify the gap between fixed and item-adaptive budget allocation, and test
whether observable prefix signals contain information about future continuation
value across models and tasks.

\end{enumerate}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\paragraph{Marginal returns of test-time compute.}
The observation that additional reasoning can exhibit diminishing or negative
returns motivates adaptive allocation of inference-time computation. Prior work
has primarily characterised such behaviour retrospectively on labelled
benchmarks or designed heuristics for terminating trajectories early. Our focus
is different: we treat the value of continuation as a deployment-time quantity
that must be estimated and updated online.

\paragraph{Reasoning and calibration.}
Increasing the reasoning budget can change not only top-1 correctness but also
the predictive distribution associated with an answer. This distinction is
important for systems that use confidence for triage, deferral, or abstention.
Rather than assuming that longer reasoning universally improves or degrades
calibration, we treat the relationship between reasoning budget and proper
scoring loss as an empirical question and measure it across budgets, models,
and tasks.

\paragraph{Training-free early exit.}
Several approaches terminate reasoning without additional model training using
trial answers, entropy, confidence trajectories, answer convergence, or
stability of intermediate predictions. These methods form important baselines
for our study. Such signals may indicate trajectory stabilisation, but without
additional assumptions they do not resolve the non-identifiability of current
correctness established in Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility}.

\paragraph{Statistical guarantees for stopping.}
Distribution-free risk control, calibration procedures, and sequential testing
provide principled mechanisms for selecting prediction or stopping rules, but
typically rely on labelled calibration data sampled before deployment. We study
a different regime in which no separate calibration split is reserved and
labels instead arrive sequentially from the deployment stream.

\paragraph{Anytime-valid inference.}
Confidence sequences and e-processes provide statistical guarantees that remain
valid under continuous monitoring and optional stopping. These tools are
particularly natural for deployment-time budget selection because the evidence
used to choose a policy accumulates over time and the policy itself may be
updated whenever sufficient evidence becomes available.

