\section{Preliminaries and an Impossibility Result}
\label{sec:impossibility}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Fix a query $x$ with ground-truth answer $y^\star$. A reasoning model produces
a trajectory $r_{1:\Bmax}$, and at each checkpoint $\budget$ we may force
termination and read off a candidate answer $\hat{y}_\budget$ together with a
predictive distribution $\hat{p}_\budget \in \Delta^{C-1}$. Write the
observable history as

\begin{equation}
\Hist_\budget =
\bigl(
x,\;
r_{1:\budget},\;
\text{logits}_{1:\budget},\;
\hat{y}_\budget,\;
\hat{p}_\budget
\bigr),
\end{equation}

and the unobserved correctness indicator as

\begin{equation}
Y_\budget = \Ind\{\hat{y}_\budget = y^\star\}.
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Non-identifiability of label-free correctness]
\label{prop:impossibility}
Let $f$ be any measurable estimator
$\hat{a}_\budget=f(\Hist_\budget)$.
There exist two joint distributions $\Prob_0,\Prob_1$ over
$(\Hist_\budget,Y_\budget)$ with identical marginals on $\Hist_\budget$ such
that $Y_\budget=0$ almost surely under $\Prob_0$ and $Y_\budget=1$ almost surely
under $\Prob_1$. Consequently, for every $\Hist_\budget$,

\begin{equation}
\max
\left\{
\left|\hat{a}_\budget-0\right|,
\left|\hat{a}_\budget-1\right|
\right\}
\geq \frac{1}{2},
\end{equation}

and no non-trivial distribution-free finite-sample lower bound on

\begin{equation}
\Prob(Y_\budget=1\mid\Hist_\budget)
\end{equation}

exists.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Construct $\Prob_1$ from $\Prob_0$ by relabelling $y^\star$ to agree with
$\hat{y}_\budget$ on every realisation. Since $y^\star$ enters
$\Hist_\budget$ nowhere, the marginal law of $\Hist_\budget$ is unchanged, so
$f$ and any stopping rule measurable with respect to $\Hist_\budget$ behave
identically under both distributions. The stated bound follows pointwise.
\TODO{Provide the complete proof in Appendix A.}
\end{proof}

The obstruction is informational rather than statistical: collecting additional
unlabelled trajectories cannot remove it without introducing assumptions that
connect observable model behaviour to ground-truth correctness.
Self-consistency, answer stability, and related trajectory-based quantities are
therefore useful descriptive signals but do not constitute correctness
guarantees on their own.

\begin{remark}
Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} does not preclude label-free detection of
\emph{trajectory stabilisation}, including answer convergence, entropy
plateaus, or representational saturation. It only precludes attaching a
distribution-free correctness or calibration guarantee to such behaviour
without additional assumptions or labelled information.
\end{remark}

