\section{The Marginal Value of Thinking}
\label{sec:formulation}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\subsection{Loss}

Let

\begin{equation}
\budget_1 < \cdots < \budget_K = \Bmax
\end{equation}

be a globally fixed budget grid. For item $i$ at checkpoint $j$ we use the
multiclass Brier score

\begin{equation}
\Brier_{i,j}
=
\left\lVert
\hat{p}_{i,j}-e_{y_i}
\right\rVert_2^2 ,
\end{equation}

and, writing $\cost_{i,j}$ for realised compute, define the deployment loss

\begin{equation}
\lossfn^{(\lambda)}_{i,j}
=
\Brier_{i,j}
+
\lambda\,\cost_{i,j},
\qquad
\lambda\geq0.
\end{equation}

Here $\lambda$ is not treated as a hyperparameter selected to maximise benchmark
performance. It represents a user-specified exchange rate between predictive
quality and compute. Accordingly, empirical results are reported over a range
of $\lambda$ values and as quality--compute Pareto frontiers rather than at a
single privileged operating point.

\subsection{Marginal value and its decomposition}

\begin{definition}[Marginal value of a reasoning block]
The marginal value of continuing from checkpoint $j$ to $j+1$ is

\begin{equation}
\margval_j(\lambda)
=
\Exp\left[
\Brier_{i,j}
-
\Brier_{i,j+1}
\right]
-
\lambda
\Exp\left[
\cost_{i,j+1}
-
\cost_{i,j}
\right].
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

Applying the reliability--resolution--uncertainty decomposition of the Brier
score,

\begin{equation}
\Exp[\Brier_j]
=
\mathrm{REL}_j
-
\mathrm{RES}_j
+
\mathrm{UNC},
\end{equation}

and noting that the uncertainty term depends only on the label distribution and
therefore cancels across budgets, yields

\begin{equation}
\margval_j(\lambda)
=
\underbrace{
\left(
\mathrm{RES}_{j+1}-\mathrm{RES}_j
\right)
}_{\text{change in resolution}}
-
\underbrace{
\left(
\mathrm{REL}_{j+1}-\mathrm{REL}_j
\right)
}_{\text{change in reliability error}}
-
\lambda\Delta\cost_j .
\label{eq:decomposition}
\end{equation}

Equation~\eqref{eq:decomposition} separates two ways in which an additional
reasoning block can change probabilistic quality. It may improve resolution,
change reliability error, or both. Their relative magnitudes can vary across
models, datasets, and budgets; accordingly, we make no universal claim that
longer reasoning must improve or degrade calibration. Determining the empirical
shape of these terms is one purpose of the budget audit in
Section~\ref{sec:audit}.

