\section{MVT-CS}
\label{sec:method}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\subsection{Scope}

\begin{assumption}[Delayed verifiable feedback]
\label{asm:feedback}
For each item $i$, the outcome $y_i$ becomes available after a finite delay
through exact-answer matching, program execution, formal verification, or
post-hoc adjudication.
\end{assumption}

Assumption~\ref{asm:feedback} delimits the scope of the method. Open-ended
generation for which no outcome ever becomes available is outside the setting
considered here.

\subsection{Randomized full-trajectory audits}

If a deployed policy stops an item early, predictions that would have been
obtained at larger budgets are not observed. To reveal these counterfactuals,
we randomly select a subset of deployment items for full-trajectory auditing.

Let

\begin{equation}
A_i \sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(\rho_i),
\end{equation}

where audited items are executed through $\Bmax$ and all checkpoint predictions
are retained. Because one full trajectory exposes the predictions encountered
by every candidate stopping policy in a fixed policy family, the same audited
item can be used to evaluate multiple policies.

For a policy $\pi$, let

\begin{equation}
\tau_\pi(\Hist_i)
\end{equation}

denote the checkpoint at which that policy would stop. Define its realised loss
on item $i$ as

\begin{equation}
\ell_i(\pi)
=
\Brier_{i,\tau_\pi}
+
\lambda
\cost_{i,\tau_\pi}.
\end{equation}

Relative to a reference policy $\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}$, an importance-weighted
observation is

\begin{equation}
X_i(\pi)
=
\frac{A_i}{\rho_i}
\left[
\ell_i(\pi)
-
\ell_i(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}})
\right].
\label{eq:policy-ipw}
\end{equation}

Under randomized auditing,

\begin{equation}
\Exp[
X_i(\pi)
\mid
\mathcal{F}_{i-1}
]
=
R(\pi)-R(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}),
\end{equation}

where

\begin{equation}
R(\pi)=\Exp[\ell_i(\pi)].
\end{equation}

The audit probability $\rho_i$ may itself depend on past observations, provided
it is determined before the current item's counterfactual outcome is revealed.
All audit computation is included in the reported total compute.

\subsection{Anytime-valid policy evaluation}

Because deployment proceeds sequentially, policy comparisons are repeatedly
inspected and may trigger decisions at data-dependent times. We therefore
maintain simultaneous confidence sequences for the excess risk of every policy
relative to the reference.

For a finite policy family $\Pi$, the target guarantee is

\begin{equation}
\Prob
\left(
\forall n,\;
\forall\pi\in\Pi:
R(\pi)-R(\pi_{\mathrm{ref}})
\in
[
\underline{\Delta}_{\pi,n},
\overline{\Delta}_{\pi,n}
]
\right)
\geq
1-\alpha .
\end{equation}

The intervals can be constructed using bounded empirical-Bernstein confidence
sequences or betting-based methods, with simultaneous coverage across the
finite policy family.

One deployment rule is to retain policies whose upper confidence bounds remain
compatible with a user-specified tolerance $\epsilon$ relative to the reference,
then select the lowest-compute policy among them. Equivalently, racing can be
used to eliminate policies that are statistically dominated by another active
candidate.

\subsection{Policy family}

We pre-specify a finite family of stopping rules. Candidate policies may include

\begin{itemize}
\item fixed-budget policies;
\item predictive-confidence thresholds;
\item entropy thresholds;
\item answer-stability rules;
\item top-1/top-2 margin thresholds;
\item confidence-slope or probability-dynamics thresholds.
\end{itemize}

For a scalar signal $s_{i,j}$ observed at checkpoint $j$, a generic threshold
policy can be written as

\begin{equation}
\tau_\theta(\Hist_i)
=
\min
\left\{
j:
s_{i,j}\leq\theta_j
\right\},
\qquad
\theta\in\Theta,
\qquad
|\Theta|<\infty.
\end{equation}

Every audited full trajectory permits reconstruction of the stopping checkpoint
and realised loss for every policy in this finite family.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{MVT-CS: prequential reasoning-budget selection}
\label{alg:mvtcs}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]

\State \textbf{Input:}
budget grid $\{\budget_j\}_{j=1}^{K}$,
policy family $\Pi$,
exchange rate $\lambda$,
confidence level $\alpha$,
audit schedule $\{\rho_i\}$

\State Initialise confidence sequences for all $\pi\in\Pi$

\For{each arriving item $i$}

    \State Select the currently preferred active policy $\pi_i$

    \State Execute the reasoning process according to $\pi_i$

    \State Draw
    $A_i\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(\rho_i)$
    before observing the delayed outcome

    \If{$A_i=1$}
        \State Continue the item to $\Bmax$ and retain all checkpoint predictions
    \EndIf

    \State Deploy the answer produced by $\pi_i$

    \State \textbf{When $y_i$ arrives:}

    \If{$A_i=1$}
        \State Reconstruct $\ell_i(\pi)$ for all $\pi\in\Pi$
        \State Form importance-weighted policy losses using
        Eq.~\eqref{eq:policy-ipw}
        \State Update all confidence sequences
        \State Eliminate statistically dominated policies if applicable
    \EndIf

\EndFor

\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{What is and is not learned}

MVT-CS performs no gradient update to the reasoning language model and trains no
auxiliary correctness or confidence predictor. It does, however, learn from the
deployment stream: policy-risk estimates, confidence sequences, and the set of
active candidate policies are data-dependent. We therefore describe the
procedure as \emph{held-out-free} rather than \emph{training-free}.

