\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experiments}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\subsection{Models and infrastructure}

We evaluate \TODO{$M$ reasoning models} with native or explicit reasoning
modes. The primary measurement protocol uses bfloat16 model weights and
bfloat16 key--value caches because probability-level calibration analyses are
sensitive to numerical perturbations.

Experiments run on a single workstation equipped with one NVIDIA RTX 4090 and
two RTX 3090 GPUs, each with 24\,GB of memory and without NVLink. Models at the
target scale fit on individual GPUs, so inference uses independent data-parallel
processes rather than tensor parallelism.

Before pooling probability-based measurements across devices, we explicitly
test logit reproducibility. Two RTX 3090 devices produced identical outputs on
the diagnostic set, whereas RTX 4090 and RTX 3090 inference exhibited
non-negligible log-probability differences. Accordingly, primary
probability-based results never mix GPU architectures within the same
model--dataset experimental cell.

Checkpoint probes reuse the generated reasoning prefix where applicable, and
generation is performed once per trajectory before downstream budget analyses.

\subsection{Datasets}

The initial multiple-choice evaluation comprises

\begin{itemize}
\item MMLU-Pro;
\item ARC-Challenge;
\item MedMCQA;
\item MedQA.
\end{itemize}

These datasets provide exact labels and well-defined predictive distributions,
which are required for Brier score and negative-log-likelihood analysis.

The initial corpus is intended to establish whether a meaningful reasoning
budget opportunity exists. If the measured curves indicate that all current
benchmarks saturate at the minimum tested budget, we separately evaluate
whether the effect is caused by an overly high budget floor or insufficient
problem difficulty before drawing conclusions about the broader research
question.

\subsection{Budget grid and forcing protocol}

The primary budget grid is

\begin{equation}
\{256,512,1024,2048,4096,8192\}
\end{equation}

reasoning tokens.

The grid is fixed before inspecting the primary results. We record the natural
termination point of each trajectory and distinguish between natural stopping
and forced checkpoint evaluation where required.

Because forced termination can itself act as an intervention on generation,
this effect is treated explicitly in the experimental protocol rather than
implicitly equating every checkpoint with an independently generated
short-budget trajectory.

\subsection{Gate 0: opportunity}

Before constructing an adaptive stopping procedure, we first test whether
adaptive computation has meaningful opportunity.

For each model--dataset cell we report

\begin{itemize}
\item accuracy at every budget;
\item Brier score at every budget;
\item negative log-likelihood at every budget;
\item mean predictive confidence;
\item answer-flip rate;
\item correct$\rightarrow$wrong transitions;
\item wrong$\rightarrow$correct transitions.
\end{itemize}

We then compare the best global fixed budget against an item-adaptive oracle.

The primary opportunity criterion is whether the adaptive oracle can reduce
compute by at least

\begin{equation}
10\%
\end{equation}

relative to the best fixed budget under matched predictive quality.

If even an oracle cannot achieve a meaningful reduction, instance-adaptive
budget selection has insufficient opportunity in that experimental regime.

\subsection{Gate 1: predictability of continuation value}

For each edge $j\rightarrow j+1$, define the item-level gain

\begin{equation}
G_{i,j}
=
\Brier_{i,j}
-
\Brier_{i,j+1}.
\end{equation}

At checkpoint $j$ we evaluate simple observable signals including

\begin{itemize}
\item confidence;
\item entropy;
\item top-1/top-2 margin;
\item answer stability;
\item confidence slope;
\item probability dynamics.
\end{itemize}

We measure both

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{AUC}
\left(
s_{i,j},
\Ind\{G_{i,j}>0\}
\right)
\end{equation}

and Spearman rank correlation between $s_{i,j}$ and $G_{i,j}$.

The pre-specified continuation criterion is

\begin{equation}
\mathrm{AUC}\geq0.60
\end{equation}

or

\begin{equation}
|\rho_{\mathrm{Spearman}}|\geq0.30
\end{equation}

on a held-out model--dataset setting.

Failure of both criteria terminates the instance-adaptive signal-based branch
of the method rather than motivating additional predictor complexity.

\subsection{Baselines}

We compare against the following classes of baselines.

\begin{itemize}

\item \textbf{Fixed budgets:}
every point in the predefined budget grid, including the dataset-level best
fixed budget.

\item \textbf{Training-free stopping signals:}
confidence thresholds, entropy thresholds, answer stability, confidence
dynamics, and related trajectory-based stopping rules.

\item \textbf{Calibration-based procedures:}
methods that select thresholds or risks using a separate labeled calibration
split.

\item \textbf{Oracle policies:}
item-level policies with access to all budget outcomes, used only to measure
available opportunity and not as deployable competitors.

\end{itemize}

\subsection{Metrics}

Primary metrics are

\begin{itemize}
\item multiclass Brier score;
\item negative log-likelihood;
\item accuracy;
\item total reasoning tokens;
\item wall-clock latency;
\item total compute including probing and randomized audits;
\item token savings at matched accuracy;
\item token savings at matched Brier score.
\end{itemize}

Secondary metrics include

\begin{itemize}
\item mean predictive confidence;
\item calibration slope and intercept;
\item correct$\rightarrow$wrong transition rate;
\item wrong$\rightarrow$correct rescue rate;
\item answer-flip rate;
\item item-level optimal-budget distribution.
\end{itemize}

For the online setting we additionally measure

\begin{itemize}
\item cumulative prequential regret;
\item audit and exploration overhead;
\item burn-in compute;
\item number of delayed outcomes required for policy stabilisation.
\end{itemize}

Expected calibration error is not treated as a primary metric because it is
sensitive to binning choices and sample size.

\subsection{Statistical protocol}

The unit of analysis is the item. We report paired uncertainty intervals over
items and preserve model--dataset cells rather than pooling measurements that
violate hardware or task comparability.

For online simulations, cached trajectories are replayed under multiple stream
orderings so that sequential adaptation can be evaluated without regenerating
the underlying model outputs.

All policy families, threshold grids, budget grids, and gate criteria are
specified before the corresponding primary evaluation.

