\paragraph{Limitations.}

Assumption~\ref{asm:feedback} restricts applicability to workloads for which
outcomes eventually become verifiable or adjudicable. Tasks involving
open-ended generation with no reliable eventual feedback fall outside the
present setting.

The policy family is finite and fixed in advance, so the method does not claim
to recover an unrestricted optimal stopping rule. Its guarantees concern the
candidate policies included in the evaluation.

Forced checkpoint evaluation may intervene on the trajectory rather than simply
observe an otherwise identical latent reasoning process. We therefore record
the forcing protocol explicitly and interpret budget comparisons within that
measurement regime.

The initial experiments are limited to models that can be evaluated reliably on
the available single-workstation hardware. Whether continuation-value patterns
change qualitatively at substantially larger model scales remains an empirical
question.

Finally, randomized auditing incurs real compute and latency costs on a subset
of deployment traffic. These costs are included in our accounting, but some
applications may impose per-request constraints that make such exploration
undesirable even when its long-run expected value is positive.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Conclusion}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

We study test-time reasoning-budget selection in a deployment regime where no
separate held-out calibration set is reserved but delayed outcomes eventually
become available. We first distinguish current correctness from the value of
continuation and show that correctness is not identifiable from an unlabeled
single trajectory without additional structural assumptions. This motivates the
\emph{marginal value of thinking}: the expected improvement in a proper scoring
loss produced by an additional reasoning block, net of its compute cost.

MVT-CS uses randomized trajectory audits and delayed deployment feedback to
evaluate a finite family of reasoning-budget policies with anytime-valid
statistical guarantees. The empirical study is deliberately structured to test
the premise required for such a method before claiming its usefulness:
reasoning budgets must exhibit meaningful item-level heterogeneity, and
observable trajectory information must contain signal about future continuation
value.

